From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Open Arc Authors <dev@openarc.invalid>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:49:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] openarc: embedder: ad-block filter-list updates without a
 restart
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

DRAFT, NOT APPLIED. Written against openarc/156 as exported in
chromium-patches/156 (0018 and later) but neither compiled nor run; the
main session applies, builds and tests it (chromium-bridge/README.md
§Ad blocking, "Applying the C19 draft").

Plan C19. The ad blocker (0018) shipped its EasyList + EasyPrivacy ruleset
only inside the framework, so new filter lists needed an app release.
The shell now downloads newer rulesets from our static host and verifies
them (Ed25519-signed manifest, size and SHA-256 per file; Swift, in
app/Sources/OpenArcEngine/AdBlockRulesetUpdates.swift) and stores them in
<user data dir>/Open Arc Ad Block/rulesets/<version>/ with an atomically
swapped "Current" link. This change lets the engine use them:

- -[OACApplication loadAdBlockingRulesetFromDirectory:completionHandler:]
  hands a verified directory to AdBlocker::LoadRuleset, which gives it to
  Chrome's RulesetService: a new content version is indexed on the
  service's background sequence and published to every renderer, so page
  loads after that use it without a restart. The handler reports the
  version once it has been handed over.
- AdBlocker::LoadRuleset takes an optional completion callback.
- At startup, LoadBundledRuleset compares the bundled ruleset with the
  downloaded one (Current, resolved and kept inside the store) and loads
  the downloaded one unless the bundled one was built on a later day. Both
  must be compared: RulesetService re-indexes whenever the supplied
  version differs from the last indexed one, so loading the older bundled
  ruleset would undo the download at every start.
- kRulesetUpdateManifestURL goes away: the URL, the schedule and the trust
  decision live in the shell, which unit-tests them.

Without this change the shell's updater stays off (it checks for the
bridge method at run time).
